Cretaceous Pterosaurs (larger, more advanced pterodactyloids; many excellent soarers)These dominate the high-speed end thanks to bigger size and better soaring adaptations.Tropeognathus — Large ornithocheirid; top dynamic soarer.

*Anhanguera — Excellent marine soarer, very fast/endurant.
Coloborhynchus — Robust ornithocheirid; strong flyer.
Ornithocheirus — Very large; high-speed potential.
*Pteranodon — Classic dynamic soarer (50–80+ km/h estimates).
Nyctosaurus — Extreme soaring specialist; potentially elite.
Thalassodromeus — Crested, fast aerial form.
Guidraco / Ludodactylus — Ornithocheirid relatives; solid speed.
*Tapejara / Tupandactylus — Tapejarids; good gliders.
Tupuxuara — Similar tapejaroid performance.
*Quetzalcoatlus — Giant azhdarchid; strong glider (high estimates in some models).
Arambourgiania — Massive azhdarchid; excellent glider.
*Hatzegopteryx — Robust giant; powerful but possibly more terrestrial.
Cryodrakon — Large azhdarchid.
Thanatosdrakon — Giant; good glider.
Zhejiangopterus — Azhdarchid.
Dsungaripterus / Noripterus — Dsungaripterids; robust medium-large.
Bakonydraco / Bangiadraco — Azhdarchoid-like.
Caiuajara — Tapejarid.
Sinopterus — Small tapejarid; agile but slower.
Pterodaustro — Filter-feeder; moderate.
Ctenochasma / Gnathosaurus — Long-jawed specialists; not speed-focused.
Boreopterus / Caupedactylus — Medium pterodactyloids.
Istiodactylus — Scavenger/predator; decent.
*Kariridraco — Smaller thalassodromine; lower end.


